!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/http/rpc-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/http/problem-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  The wire form of an interface: one method call and its return. A packet is DATA, so this is a user
  schema, not part of the meta layer -- the meta layer says what an interface IS; this says what calling
  one looks like on a wire.

  Both templates are CLOSED PER METHOD by a wire schema derived from the interface -- one entry per method,
  `place_order_call => call<new_order>`, `place_order_return => return<order, place_order_errors>` -- and a
  packet names the closed type: `!place_order_call { ... }`. That is what makes a packet fully typed: its
  request and response are checked against the interface's own types by the resolver, an error arm is one
  of the method's declared errors with its status fixed, and this schema imports no service's types. The
  wire schema is deterministic in the interface, so it is generated, published at its own identity, and
  fetched like any other; examples/orders-wire-1.tn is one written by hand to show the shape.

  Both gateways translate INTO this. An api gateway turns an HTTP request into a call by the binding's
  placement run in reverse; an agent processor executes a compiled agent (agent-vm-1.tn), each of whose
  CALL instructions is one of these. In-process Java dispatch is the first transport; a remote one is one
  more. Several calls in one request, with data flowing between them, is the agent's business: a plan
  (agent-1.tn) is the AST that compiles to it.

  The method's address is a content-addressed identity: the interface's `!!id` and the method's key,
  unambiguous across every service a gateway fronts, versioned by the identity, with no registry and no
  numeric ids -- and redundant with the closed type's name, kept because a packet should say where it is
  going without anyone parsing a type name.

  Precedents, and what each contributed: gRPC's closed set of transport-level statuses, its deadline, and
  its frames-plus-end-status model for a stream; JSON-RPC 2.0's self-contained envelope and correlation
  id; Avro's per-method declared errors, returned as typed values rather than codes. (Cap'n Proto's
  promised answers -- a call naming an earlier call's result by id and a path into it -- are the agent's
  precedent, in agent-1.tn's selectors.)
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    One method call. `interface` and `method` are the address; `id` correlates a return with its call
    where a transport multiplexes, and is redundant over unary HTTP; `deadline` is the one piece of
    transport metadata a callee must know about, since it decides whether to start; `meta` is everything
    else -- tracing, credentials -- which this schema deliberately does not describe.

    `Req` is the method's request record. A method that takes nothing closes it with `void`.
    """
  call => <Req> {
    interface: uri
    method:    text
    id:        text?
    deadline:  duration?
    meta:      {text => text}?
    request:   Req?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One outcome, and exactly one: the method's `response`, one of the `error`s its signature declared, or
    a `fault`. `Resp` is the response type; `Err` is the declared error type, or a choice of them when the
    method declares several -- a choice of records, so the value carries the error's tag, which is the
    discriminator a caller wants. An error is a typed value composing `problem`, its status pinned on the
    type, and the resolver enforces the pin. A `fault` is the transport's or the processor's own failure,
    a `problem` whose `type` comes from the same small closed set the HTTP problem types use, so an api
    gateway maps by table rather than by translation. The group is what keeps business errors and faults
    apart, which is the distinction gRPC's single status channel blurs and the one a caller deciding
    whether to retry needs.
    """
  return => <Resp, Err> {
    id: text?
    ( response: Resp | error: Err | fault: problem )
  }

  @doc:"The return of a method that declares no errors: a response, or a fault."
  return_plain => <Resp> {
    id: text?
    ( response: Resp | fault: problem )
  }
}
